# SH-10 — the idol-native owner of C SYMBOL emission.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Three separate canonical repairs — gap[083] name
# mangling, gap[087] bare-identity resolution, gap[088] constructor rung (a) —
# all terminate at ONE missing capability: there is no idol-native owner of
# code emission. `lib/compiler/` has a lexer, a parser and a projection.
# Nothing here owns a symbol.
#
# So the repair for all three is only expressible in the bootstrap, which the
# owner directive excludes. This file is the smallest piece that changes that:
# it owns the NAME, in idol, provably.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. Not a code generator. It emits no declarations, no bodies, no
# records. It answers exactly one question — what C symbol does this idol name
# take — and that is the question gap[083] is about.
#
# THE DEFECT IT CLOSES, measured 2026-08-08: a idol function named `read`,
# `write`, `open` or `abs` emitted its name UNMANGLED into C, where libc had
# already declared it. `abs(21)` compiled clean and printed **21** — libc's
# answer, not idol's. Not a build failure. A wrong number.
#
# WHY UNCONDITIONAL RATHER THAN A KEYWORD LIST. libc's namespace is OPEN: any
# symbol it declares next release can capture a idol name, so the colliding set
# is unknowable from inside the compiler. A list is a census that goes stale
# and reports a confident zero. Prefixing every root-file function needs no
# list and cannot go stale.

# The C symbol for a root-file, single-word, non-method idol function.
#
# `duo_fn_` is chosen for the reason any prefix is chosen: C reserves
# identifiers beginning `_` at file scope, and `duo_` is already this
# compiler's prefix for every other emitted symbol, so the namespace is one
# rather than two.
root: str = (name: str)
  "duo_fn_{name}"

# A module function is ALREADY closed by its module prefix — `<mod>__<name>` is
# not a spelling libc can hold, because libc has no `__` in that position. This
# is why only the root translation unit ever needed repair, and recording it
# here stops the next reader prefixing twice.
module: str = (home: str, name: str)
  "{home}__{name}"

# `main` is the one root name that may NOT take the ordinary prefix: the
# runtime driver emits its own `int main(...)` and calls this as the entry
# point. A collision here is a link error rather than a silent bind, but the
# repair is the same shape.
entry: str = ()
  "duo_entry_main"

# Whether a root name needs the prefix at all. Everything does — that IS the
# rule — and the function exists so a caller states the decision rather than
# assuming it, and so the one exception has somewhere to live.
mangles: bool = (name: str)
  if name == "main"
    return false
  true

# The C symbol for a root name, with the `main` exception applied.
symbol: str = (name: str)
  if mangles(name)
    return root(name)
  entry()
